Now put only vacuum energy on the right-hand side of the Friedmann equation. Since $\rho_{\rm vac}$ is constant, $\left(\frac{\dot a}{a}\right)^2
=\frac{8\pi G_N}{3}\rho_{\rm vac}
\equiv H^2.$ There are two uses of $H$ to keep separate. Equation  defines the possibly time-dependent quantity $\dot a/a$. Equation  says that in a pure vacuum-energy universe its value is the constant $H=\sqrt{\frac{8\pi G_N\rho_{\rm vac}}{3}}.$ Taking the expanding branch yields $\dot a=Ha,$ and therefore $a(t)=C e^{Ht}.$ The integration constant $C$ fixes the arbitrary normalization of comoving coordinates. Growth proportional to the current value of $a$ is exponential growth.

